{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/543642-cool-essay-on-how-to-run-mysteries-in-an-rpg","id":543642,"title":"Cool essay on how to run mysteries in an RPG","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-06-07T02:13:22-07:00","id":543642,"title":"Cool essay on how to run mysteries in an RPG","content":"<a href=\"http://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/1118/roleplaying-games/three-clue-rule\" target=\"_blank\">http://thealexandrian.net/wordpress/...hree-clue-rule</a><br /> <br /> TL;DR<ul><li>Identify any &quot;chokepoints&quot; in your story structure (for example, in order to proceed, the players must find a secret door)</li> <li>Either remove the chokepoint, or, write at least three clues leading to the action/conclusion you want the players to make.<ul><li>For example, an important secret door might be found with a search check, mentioned in an NPC journal, or the pc's might see an NPC use the secret door</li> </ul></li> <li>Be permissive when it comes to clue-finding. If a player does something you don't anticipate to try to solve the mystery, reward this creativity with useful information.</li> <li>Don't get married to your clues. They are a safety net. If they manage to bypass or ignore your <i>ingeeeeenious clue</i>, let it go.</li> <li>If all else fails, have a guy with a gun walk through the door.</li> <li>Leave the red herrings at the fishmonger's.</li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1078,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","user":"Froggy711","date":"06-09-2015, 05:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1078-froggy711","name":"Froggy711","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1078_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":